Typing‑practice tools are usually flat, repetitive drills — type random words against a timer — which makes them feel mechanicaland easy to disengage from, especially 
for or casual or younger learners. Generic typing games also tend to lack two things:
(1) a sense of stakes or feedback that rewards both speed and accuracy rather than just words‑per‑minute, and 
(2) difficulty that scales smoothly with the player’s skill instead of staying flat or spiking suddenly. On top of that, most typing games have no cultural or thematic identity 
   — they’re generic word lists with no hook to make the practice session feel occasion-specific or fun to return to.
